.TH MIRRORFS 1 "November 2026" "mirrorfs 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mirrorfs \- mirror local filesystem mutations onto a remote directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY mirrorfs
.B \-\-local
.I path
.B \-\-remote
.I path
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.RB [ \-\-allow-other ]
.I mountpoint
.YS
.SY mirrorfs
.I local
.I mountpoint
.B \-o
.I remote=path\fR[\fI,option\fR...]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mirrorfs
is a FUSE filesystem.
The local directory is the only source of truth.
A mutation is applied there and the call returns.
One background thread then replays a persistent queue onto the remote directory.
Reads never consult the remote.
.PP
The first form stays in the foreground and writes its log to the terminal.
Stop it with
.B SIGINT
or
.BR SIGTERM ,
or run
.BR "fusermount3 \-u" .
.PP
The second form is what
.BR mount (8)
uses for an
.B /etc/fstab
entry of type
.BR fuse.mirrorfs .
It is described in
.BR fuse.mirrorfs (8).
Without
.BR foreground ,
that form forks into the background after the mount is ready.
Its log goes to
.IR /var/log/mirrorfs.log .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-local " path"
Local directory.
Reads and the accepted state come from here.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote " path"
Directory that receives the asynchronous mirror.
It can be the root of a mount or a folder inside one.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote\-fs " type"
Kernel filesystem type that directory must be on, as printed by
.BR "findmnt \-n \-o FSTYPE" .
Examples:
.BR nfs ,
.BR cifs ,
.BR ceph .
.B nfs
also matches the kernel type
.BR nfs4 .
Repeat this option, or join several types with
.BR : .
Until the path is on one of those types, mirrorfs stays mounted and queues
mutations. It does not copy them onto the local directory that appears at
the path while the remote filesystem is absent.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote\-check " duration"
How often to look for
.B \-\-remote\-fs
while it is absent.
.BR 30s ,
.BR 5m ,
.BR 1h ,
or a bare number of seconds.
The default is
.BR 5m .
The first look happens at startup.
When the type matches, the queue is drained through a directory fd held on
that filesystem.
This option requires
.BR \-\-remote\-fs .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Log every operation: path, name, result, and whether it was queued.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-other
Let users other than the mounter access the filesystem.
The kernel still checks each file's mode, owner, and group.
Mounting as root does not need
.I user_allow_other
in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf .
Mounting as a non-root user does.
.TP
.I mountpoint
Existing directory to mount on.
It must differ from both the local directory and the remote directory.
.PP
In the
.B \-o
form, the local directory and the mount point are the two positional
arguments, and the remote directory is the
.BI remote= path
option.
That form also accepts
.BR allow_other ,
.BR allow_root ,
.BR debug ,
.BR foreground ,
.BI remote\-fs= type\fR,
and
.BI remote\-check= duration\fR,
plus the VFS flags listed in
.BR fuse.mirrorfs (8).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <local>/.mirrorfs\-queue/
Persistent queue of operations waiting for the remote directory.
The directory is hidden from the mount.
A file of 8 MiB or less is stored in its queue record.
A larger file is copied sparsely, in 1 MiB chunks, into a sibling
.I .blob
file before the queue lock is taken.
Holes are not written out.
The worker replays that blob the same way.
.TP
.I <mount>/sys/stats
.TQ
.I <mount>/sys/queue
.TQ
.I <mount>/sys/errors
Userspace status files.
They are not kernel sysfs entries.
The directory is omitted from listings of the mount root, including
.BR "ls \-a" .
Opening
.I <mount>/sys
still works, and the files inside are listed once it is open.
The directory is mode 0555 and the files are mode 0444.
.TP
.I /var/log/mirrorfs.log
Log for a daemonized mount.
If the file cannot be opened, the log is discarded.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on a clean unmount, or when a daemonized parent has seen the mount succeed.
.B 1
when the arguments, a directory, or the mount itself is rejected.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fuse.mirrorfs (8),
.BR mount (8),
.BR mount.fuse3 (8),
.BR fusermount3 (1)
